{"root_cause":"The bug is in the module-level variable _default_host and the function get_host. _default_host is initialized once at import time as _settings["host"] (the line "_default_host = _settings["host"]"), so get_host() returns that captured value instead of reading the live _settings. Because _default_host is never updated, get_host() becomes stale after configure() or reset_settings() change _settings.","failure_mechanism":"Concrete scenario: on import, _default_host becomes "localhost". If later code calls configure(host="example.com"), configure() updates _settings["host"] to "example.com" but does not touch _default_host. A subsequent call to get_host() returns the original captured "localhost" (because get_host() returns _default_host) even though get_settings()["host"] would return "example.com". This shows the eager capture causes a mismatch between get_host() and the actual settings.","broken_invariant":"The module promises that get_host() reflects the current host in the settings; equivalently, for any time t after configuration calls: get_host() == get_settings()["host"]. That invariant is testable and is violated by the current code when configure() or reset_settings() change the host.","fix_strategy":"I removed the eager captured _default_host and made get_host() read the host from _settings at call time. I also changed reset_settings() to mutate the existing _settings dict in-place (clear() and update(DEFAULTS)) rather than reassigning a new dict; this preserves any external references to the dict and keeps behavior consistent with configure() which mutates in-place. These changes ensure get_host() always reads the current value and that resets/configures are visible to callers.

